### What is Union-Find and when do you need it?

Union-Find, also called Disjoint Set Union (DSU), solves the dynamic connectivity problem: given a sequence of union operations that merge groups, efficiently answer whether two elements are in the same group. It supports two operations: `find(x)` returns the representative root of the set containing x, and `union(x, y)` merges the sets of x and y.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class DSU {
    private int[] parent, rank;

    public DSU(int n) {
        parent = new int[n];
        rank = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;
    }

    public int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]);
        return parent[x];
    }

    public boolean union(int x, int y) {
        int rx = find(x), ry = find(y);
        if (rx == ry) return false;
        if (rank[rx] < rank[ry]) parent[rx] = ry;
        else if (rank[rx] > rank[ry]) parent[ry] = rx;
        else { parent[ry] = rx; rank[rx]++; }
        return true;
    }

    public boolean connected(int x, int y) { return find(x) == find(y); }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        DSU dsu = new DSU(6);
        dsu.union(0, 1);
        dsu.union(2, 3);
        dsu.union(4, 5);
        System.out.println("0-1: " + dsu.connected(0, 1)); // true
        System.out.println("0-3: " + dsu.connected(0, 3)); // false
        dsu.union(1, 3);
        System.out.println("0-3: " + dsu.connected(0, 3)); // true
    }
}
```

```text
0-1: true
0-3: false
0-3: true
```

### How does path compression work internally?

Path compression happens during `find`. The recursive form `if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]); return parent[x];` walks to the root and then, as the recursion unwinds, sets every node's parent directly to the root. After one find, the entire chain is flat, so future finds on any of those nodes are O(1).

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class PathCompressionDemo {
    static int[] parent = {0, 0, 1, 2, 3}; // chain: 4->3->2->1->0

    static int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]);
        return parent[x];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Before find(4): " + Arrays.toString(parent));
        find(4);
        System.out.println("After find(4):  " + Arrays.toString(parent));
    }
}
```

```text
Before find(4): [0, 0, 1, 2, 3]
After find(4):  [0, 0, 0, 0, 0]
```

### How does union by rank work?

Union by rank keeps trees shallow. When merging two sets, find both roots. If they have different ranks (upper bounds on height), attach the lower-rank root under the higher-rank root. If they have the same rank, pick one arbitrarily and increment its rank. This ensures the tree height grows only logarithmically.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class UnionByRankDemo {
    private int[] parent, rank;

    public UnionByRankDemo(int n) {
        parent = new int[n]; rank = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;
    }

    public int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]);
        return parent[x];
    }

    public void union(int x, int y) {
        int rx = find(x), ry = find(y);
        if (rx == ry) return;
        if (rank[rx] < rank[ry]) parent[rx] = ry;
        else if (rank[rx] > rank[ry]) parent[ry] = rx;
        else { parent[ry] = rx; rank[rx]++; }
        System.out.println("union(" + x + "," + y + "): rank of root=" + rank[find(x)]);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        UnionByRankDemo uf = new UnionByRankDemo(5);
        uf.union(0, 1); // rank[0] = 1
        uf.union(2, 3); // rank[2] = 1
        uf.union(0, 2); // tie, rank[0] = 2
        uf.union(0, 4); // rank stays 2
    }
}
```

```text
union(0,1): rank of root=1
union(2,3): rank of root=1
union(0,2): rank of root=2
union(0,4): rank of root=2
```

### What is the time complexity and why?

With both path compression and union by rank, the amortised cost per operation is the inverse Ackermann function, alpha(n). For any realistic input size (up to 10^80), alpha(n) is at most 4. This makes each operation effectively constant time. Either optimisation alone gives O(log n), but together they achieve the tighter bound.

### How is Union-Find used in cycle detection?

When building a graph edge by edge, if both endpoints of a new edge already share the same root, adding that edge would create a cycle. Union-Find detects this in near-constant time per edge.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class GraphCycleDetection {
    private int[] parent, rank;

    public GraphCycleDetection(int n) {
        parent = new int[n]; rank = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;
    }

    public int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]);
        return parent[x];
    }

    public boolean addEdge(int u, int v) {
        int ru = find(u), rv = find(v);
        if (ru == rv) return false; // cycle!
        if (rank[ru] < rank[rv]) parent[ru] = rv;
        else if (rank[ru] > rank[rv]) parent[rv] = ru;
        else { parent[rv] = ru; rank[ru]++; }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        GraphCycleDetection g = new GraphCycleDetection(4);
        System.out.println("Add 0-1: " + g.addEdge(0, 1)); // true
        System.out.println("Add 1-2: " + g.addEdge(1, 2)); // true
        System.out.println("Add 2-3: " + g.addEdge(2, 3)); // true
        System.out.println("Add 3-0: " + g.addEdge(3, 0)); // false (cycle)
    }
}
```

```text
Add 0-1: true
Add 1-2: true
Add 2-3: true
Add 3-0: false
```

### How is Union-Find used in counting connected components?

After processing all edges, the number of connected components equals the number of distinct roots. You can count them by checking how many elements satisfy `parent[i] == i` (after path compression via find on each element).

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class ConnectedComponents {
    private int[] parent, rank;
    private int components;

    public ConnectedComponents(int n) {
        parent = new int[n]; rank = new int[n]; components = n;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;
    }

    public int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]);
        return parent[x];
    }

    public void union(int x, int y) {
        int rx = find(x), ry = find(y);
        if (rx == ry) return;
        if (rank[rx] < rank[ry]) parent[rx] = ry;
        else if (rank[rx] > rank[ry]) parent[ry] = rx;
        else { parent[ry] = rx; rank[rx]++; }
        components--;
    }

    public int getComponents() { return components; }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ConnectedComponents cc = new ConnectedComponents(6);
        cc.union(0, 1);
        cc.union(2, 3);
        cc.union(4, 5);
        System.out.println("Components: " + cc.getComponents()); // 3
        cc.union(1, 3);
        System.out.println("Components: " + cc.getComponents()); // 2
    }
}
```

```text
Components: 3
Components: 2
```

### What are the limitations?

Union-Find is not easily decremental. Once two sets are merged, you cannot efficiently split them apart. It suits problems where sets only grow, like accumulating fraud links. For dynamic split/merge, link-cut trees or rollback-capable DSU are needed.

### What is the banking use case?

At National Bank of India, Pankaj uses Union-Find to cluster accounts into fraud rings. Each shared signal (device, phone, transfer) triggers a union. Each risk check is a find-and-compare query. Because path compression keeps the structure flat, queries stay near-instant even as Rajesh's rings grow into the thousands of accounts.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class AccountCluster {
    private int[] parent, rank;

    public AccountCluster(int maxAccounts) {
        parent = new int[maxAccounts]; rank = new int[maxAccounts];
        for (int i = 0; i < maxAccounts; i++) parent[i] = i;
    }

    public int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]);
        return parent[x];
    }

    public void link(int a, int b) {
        int ra = find(a), rb = find(b);
        if (ra == rb) return;
        if (rank[ra] < rank[rb]) parent[ra] = rb;
        else if (rank[ra] > rank[rb]) parent[rb] = ra;
        else { parent[rb] = ra; rank[ra]++; }
    }

    public boolean sameCluster(int a, int b) { return find(a) == find(b); }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AccountCluster ac = new AccountCluster(1000);
        ac.link(101, 202); // shared device
        ac.link(202, 303); // shared phone
        System.out.println("101-303 same cluster: " + ac.sameCluster(101, 303));
        System.out.println("101-404 same cluster: " + ac.sameCluster(101, 404));
    }
}
```

```text
101-303 same cluster: true
101-404 same cluster: false
```
